f:Ta68,{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What is Japa meditation?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Japa is the repetition of a sacred name, syllable, or mantra — either audibly, in a whisper, or mentally. The Bhagavad Gita (10.25) declares Japa to be the highest of all sacrifices."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How many times should I repeat a mantra?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The traditional count is 108 repetitions, which is why a Japa Mala (rosary) has 108 beads. One full round of the Mala equals one cycle of 108 repetitions. Beginners can start with one round per sitting."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the best mantra for beginners?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Om Namah Shivaya, Om Namo Narayanaya, and So'ham (I am That) are widely recommended for beginners. The best mantra is one that resonates with your temperament. Ideally, receive one from a qualified teacher."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"When is the best time to practice Japa?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Brahma Muhurta — the period 90 minutes before sunrise — is considered the most potent time for Japa. At minimum, a consistent time each day builds the practice's power through habit."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the difference between Vaikhari, Upanshu, and Manasika Japa?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Vaikhari is audible chanting, Upanshu is whispered Japa (lips moving, barely audible), and Manasika is purely mental repetition. The tradition holds that mental Japa is the most powerful, but audible Japa is recommended for beginners to maintain focus."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I do Japa without a teacher?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, you can begin with a universal mantra like Om or So'ham without initiation. However, receiving a mantra from a qualified teacher (Diksha) is considered more potent in many traditions."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is a Japa Mala?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A Japa Mala is a string of 108 beads used to count mantra repetitions. It is held in the right hand, with the thumb moving the beads. When you reach the Meru bead (the central anchor bead), you reverse direction for the next round."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How long does it take to see results from Japa?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Noticeable effects — greater mental stillness, sense of inner peace — often appear within 40 days of consistent daily practice. The tradition prescribes a 40-day Sadhana as the minimum committed period for a new practice."}}]}7:["$","$1","c",{"children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"min-h-screen bg-background text-foreground selection:bg-orange-500/30 selection:text-orange-100 flex flex-col","children":[["$","$Le",null,{"slug":"how-to-start-japa","pillar":"practical-practices"}],["$","script",null,{"type":"application/ld+json","dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"$f"}}],["$","script",null,{"type":"application/ld+json","dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"{\"@context\":\"https://schema.org\",\"@type\":\"Article\",\"headline\":\"How to Start Japa Meditation: A Complete Step-by-Step Guide\",\"description\":\"Japa is the practice of sacred mantra repetition — one of the most accessible and powerful forms of meditation in Sanatan Dharma. Learn how to start, which mantra to choose, and how to build a daily practice.\",\"url\":\"https://opensadhaka.com/how-to-start-japa\",\"mainEntityOfPage\":\"https://opensadhaka.com/how-to-start-japa\",\"datePublished\":\"2026-03-04\",\"dateModified\":\"2026-03-04\",\"articleSection\":\"Practical Practices\",\"keywords\":[\"how to start japa meditation\"],\"about\":[{\"@type\":\"Thing\",\"name\":\"how to start japa meditation\"}],\"isPartOf\":{\"@type\":\"WebPage\",\"name\":\"Practical Practices\",\"url\":\"https://opensadhaka.com/practical-spiritual-practices\"},\"inLanguage\":\"en\",\"author\":{\"@type\":\"Organization\",\"name\":\"Sadhaka\"},\"publisher\":{\"@type\":\"Organization\",\"name\":\"Sadhaka\",\"url\":\"https://opensadhaka.com\"}}"}}],"$L10","$L11","$L12"]}],["$L13","$L14"],"$L15"]}]
